course: dictionary
lesson: 2727
title: Reading a giant word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are names, and you may not know them yet. When you meet one, read the words around it: they are its context, and they leave clues. «Diplodocus was a huge plant-eating dinosaur with a very long neck.» Huge, plant-eating and dinosaur tell you what kind of animal it was.
explain: Size clues help too. «Diplodocus was about 25 metres long, longer than a tennis court.» A tennis court is short, so this animal must have been very long. Then check your meaning by putting it in the sentence in place of the hard word.
explain: Some names are nicknames. Scientists and museums give animals long names, and people shorten them. A famous skeleton called Dippy stood in the Natural History Museum in London. Dippy is a short, friendly nickname for a Diplodocus.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Diplodocus» is the name of one kind of dinosaur, so it starts with a capital letter.
fact: «Diplodocus» means a huge plant-eating dinosaur with a very long neck and a whip-like tail.
fact: «Diplodocus» lived about 150 million years ago in North America.
fact: A sauropod is a long-necked, plant-eating dinosaur, and Diplodocus was one of them.
exercise: «Diplodocus was about 25 metres long, mostly neck and tail.» What did Diplodocus eat? | Meat from other dinosaurs | Eggs from birds | Fish from the sea | *Plants and leaves
exercise: «Diplodocus was about 25 metres long, longer than a tennis court.» What does this tell you? | It was very small | It lived on a tennis court | It had a short neck | *It was very long
exercise: «A famous skeleton nicknamed Dippy stood in the Natural History Museum.» What does nicknamed mean? | A name in another language | A name given by the law | A number on a ticket | *A friendly, short name
test: «Diplodocus lived about 150 million years ago in North America.» Where did it live? | Australia | Africa | *North America | Antarctica
test: «Diplodocus had a very long neck and an even longer whip-like tail.» What does whip-like suggest? | Short and fat | *Long, thin and bendy like a whip | Covered in bright feathers | Thick and heavy like a tree trunk
exam: «Sauropods were long-necked plant-eating dinosaurs and the biggest animals ever to walk on land.» Using these clues, what is a sauropod? | *A long-necked, plant-eating dinosaur | A type of museum building | A kind of bird that cannot fly | A fish that lives in rivers
